Employment in every major industry changed little. Health care added 17,000, slower than its average monthly gain of 33,000 over the prior 12 months. Ambulatory care added 13,000 and hospitals 12,000. Nursing and residential care lost 9,000. Construction added 11,000. Manufacturing added 9,000 and is up by 72,000 since a recent low in December 2025. Financial activities lost 7,000 and are down 129,000 since a peak in May 2025, most of that in insurance carriers and related activities.
1
record
The one-month diffusion index for 250 private industries was 49.0 in September after 57.6 in August. The release counts it as the percent of industries with employment increasing plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment, and says 50 percent is an equal balance between industries adding and cutting jobs.
1
Private payrolls rose by 46,000 and government payrolls fell by 17,000. Information fell by 10,000. Temporary help services fell by 10.9 thousand.
1
record
Average hourly earnings edged up 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81. Over the year they are up 3.0 percent. The average workweek stayed at 34.4 hours. A change of about 122,000 jobs is what the bureau calls statistically significant in the employer survey, so the 29,000 gain does not clear that bar. The household survey's bar is about 650,000.
1
record
The Associated Press called that yearly wage gain “the smallest year-over-year gain since May 2021”.
22
checked
CNBC wrote that the average workweek was unchanged at 34.6 hours. CNBC's 34.6 hours is not the release's figure. The release says the workweek remained at 34.4 hours.
17
1
checked
The unemployment rate for Black workers rose to 7.0 percent. The bureau said that rate increased, and that the rates for adult men (3.9 percent), adult women (3.6 percent), teenagers (14.5 percent), and White (3.6), Asian (2.9), and Hispanic (4.7) workers showed little change. The labor force participation rate was 61.8 percent and the employment-population ratio was 59.2 percent. The labor force rose by 485,000 and household employment by 406,000.
1
record
Job losers and people who completed temporary jobs fell by 45,000. Job leavers fell by 173,000. Reentrants rose by 152,000 and new entrants rose by 116,000. The summary table counts those groups in thousands.
1
record
The long-term unemployed, out of work 27 weeks or more, were essentially unchanged at 1.9 million and were 27.1 percent of unemployed people. People employed part time for economic reasons were little changed at 4.5 million. They would have preferred full-time employment but were working part time because their hours had been reduced or they were unable to find full-time jobs.
1
People marginally attached to the labor force fell by 236,000 to 1.5 million. Discouraged workers were little changed at 414,000.
1
record
The broader underemployment rate, U-6, was 7.6 percent in September, after 7.7 percent in August.
1
11
record
The household survey counted 8,986,000 people working more than one job in September, 5.5 percent of everyone employed, against 8,805,000 in August. The table counts those people in thousands.
2
record
Prime-age participation was 83.7 percent in September, up from 83.4 percent in August.
12
official data
On the hourly-earnings series, September's $37.81 is 3.02 percent above September 2025. The release rounds that change to 3.0 percent.
