1. **Major · new: “The Fed has decided nothing since September 16 and meets next on October 27-28.”** These are the [page bytes](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html). A [September 30 Federal Reserve Board release](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20260930a.htm) says the Board “finalized changes” through “two final rules.” The pinned [FOMC calendar](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/fomc-calendar-2026-09-16.txt) supports the October 27-28 meeting date, but only for that committee. The correct account is that the FOMC had made no further **rate decision**; the broader claim that the Fed had decided nothing is false.

2. **Minor · new: the page credits an outlet for averages available in its pinned primary data.** Page bytes: “the Associated Press put average gains at 68,000 jobs a month so far this year, and 2025 at 9,700 jobs a month.” The pinned [PAYEMS series](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/PAYEMS-2026-10-03.csv:1033) gives `2024-12-01,158316`, `2025-12-01,158432`, and `2026-09-01,159044`. Those levels yield about 9,700 per month in 2025 and exactly 68,000 per month through September 2026. The [AP pin](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/coverage/ap-september-jobs.txt) states the same averages; it is not a separate data lineage for them.

VERDICT: NO-SHIP
